  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Wednesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: The upper level low continues to travel\nacross Nevada, bringing strong winds across the forecast area. \nThe strongest winds have been recorded in White Pine county, \ngusting around 45 mph, but gusts of 35 mph have been reported \nacross Northeastern Nevada. Winds are expected to remain strong \nthrough sunset, and some blowing dust is possible as a result. \nGusty winds return tomorrow, but with peak gusts around 25 to 30 \nmph as the low exits to the east. The low will also be responsible\nfor some isolated showers and thunderstorms through Friday night,\nmainly along the northern and eastern state borders. \nAdditionally, tomorrow is the coolest day of the current forecast,\nwith highs in the upper 60s and low 70s. The low\u2019s exit marks a \ntemporary end to precipitation potential as the moisture exits \nwith it, leaving a dry and warming weekend. Isolated showers \nreturn for Monday and Tuesday due to a weak Pacific low traversing\nCalifornia and Nevada. This low will move much quicker, exiting \nthe region by Wednesday morning, leaving behind southwest flow and\nnear normal temperatures to end the work week and the forecast \nperiod.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence in strong winds\ntoday. Moderate confidence in showers and thunderstorms today and\ntomorrow. Winds and PoPs were increased through tomorrow night to\naccount for an NBM low winds bias and under representation of\nprecipitation potential during this period.",
